※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=42 diag=none

※ Assigning through a mask writes one value to every element the mask picked
※ and to nothing else.  A mask of a table's rows writes whole rows, by the same
※ rule that makes a mask of them pick whole rows.
※
※ Nothing branches here either.  What is written to each element is the old
※ value where the mask did not pick it and the new one where it did, chosen with
※ the mask spread across the whole width of the element -- all ones where it
※ picked and all zeros where it did not -- so that no instruction depends on
※ which.  Every element is written either way, which for a place the program
※ already owns is a write of what was already there.

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    let v: mut u8⟦4⟧ = ⟦10u8, 20u8, 30u8, 40u8⟧
    let want: bool⟦4⟧ = ⟦false, true, false, true⟧
    v⟦want⟧ ← 1u8
    ※ a mask worked out on the spot, over the array being written
    let w: mut u8⟦4⟧ = ⟦10u8, 20u8, 30u8, 40u8⟧
    w⟦w > 25u8⟧ ← 1u8
    ※ whole rows of a table
    let m: mut u8⟦3,2⟧ = ⟦⟦1u8, 2u8⟧, ⟦3u8, 4u8⟧, ⟦5u8, 6u8⟧⟧
    let middle: bool⟦3⟧ = ⟦false, true, false⟧
    m⟦middle⟧ ← 9u8
    ※ and an array of truth values, whose elements are one bit wide
    let b: mut bool⟦3⟧ = ⟦false, false, false⟧
    b⟦⟦true, false, true⟧⟧ ← true
    let counted: u8 = v⟦0⟧ + v⟦1⟧ + w⟦2⟧ + w⟦3⟧ + m⟦1,0⟧ + m⟦1,1⟧ + m⟦2,1⟧
    if b⟦0⟧ ∧ ¬b⟦1⟧ ∧ b⟦2⟧:
        ※ 10 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 9 + 9 + 6 = 37
        counted + 5u8
    else:
        0u8
